          "An alert rule is based on one of four threshold types: utilization percentage, minutes remaining (falling below the threshold), minutes used, or going over the allowance. Each rule can have recipients on Ankora's side, recipients on the client's side, or both.",
          "The system checks the rules automatically, immediately after every action that changes time (stopping a timer, a manual entry, an edit, a delete, or a manual adjustment to the hour bank), and also once a day as a scheduled check covering all clients.",
          "To avoid a flood of repeated emails, a rule that has already fired on crossing a threshold is not sent again until usage drops back below the threshold (\"Resolved\" when closed by hand, and \"Closed automatically\" with the reason when it closed by itself), unless you checked \"Allow the alert again after it's resolved\" for it.",
          "An alert closes automatically when a new cycle opens, when hours are loaded into the bank, when usage falls below the threshold, or when an alert for a higher threshold is created. The reason (\"A new cycle opened\", \"Hours were loaded into the bank\", \"Usage fell below the threshold\", \"An N% alert was created\") appears in the history. An alert closed by hand is not touched.",
          "Alert events not yet handled are gathered at the top of the screen under \"Needs a decision\", with \"Mark as handled\" (closes the event, and can be undone immediately) and a shortcut to open that client's hour bank. When there are no open alerts, the screen says so explicitly, together with the number of active rules and the number of clients they watch.",
